Summary
=======

This file contains instructions on how to buid Eliot on various platforms.
In case of compilation problem, you can send a mail to the eliot-dev@nongnu.org
mailing-list.

1) General information: useful information for any platform
2) Linux/Unix build
3) Windows build
4) Mac OS X build



1) General information
======================

In the following, do not forget that the cmake configuration command
can take options.
Run "cmake -B build -LH | grep ENABLE" to have the list of available options.

Here are the external dependencies of Eliot, with a link to the relevant website.
You need to install them (at least the mandatory ones) before building Eliot.
Note that the OS-specific instructions may contain hints on how to install them easily.

 - libboost (mandatory): http://www.boost.org/
   --> Used for many things

 - pugixml (mandatory): https://pugixml.org/
   --> Used for saving/loading games

 - spdlog (mandatory): https://github.com/gabime/spdlog
   --> Used for logging

 - libconfig++ (optional): http://www.hyperrealm.com/libconfig/
   --> Used for persisting preferences to disk

 - Qt (optional, but strongly recommended): https://www.qt.io/development/qt-framework
   --> Used for the graphical interface (see below)

 - libncursesw (optional): http://www.gnu.org/software/ncurses/
   --> Used for the ncurses interface (see below)

There are in fact several interfaces to Eliot:
 - a Qt interface: the best interface to use, it supports all the features.
   This one is enabled by default.
 - one in text mode: mostly useful to debug Eliot
 - one using the ncursesw library: nice and almost complete, but not really
   graphical

These interfaces can be enabled or disabled at configuration time. Example:
    cmake -B build -DENABLE_QT6=OFF -DENABLE_NCURSES=ON -DENABLE_TEXT=OFF



2) Linux/Unix build
===================

* Install the dependencies
On Debian-based systems (like Ubuntu), the following command should install
the needed dependencies (you may need to adjust the version numbers):
    sudo apt install -y cmake libboost-dev libconfig++-dev libspdlog-dev libpugixml-dev qt6-base-dev
On RPM-based systems, the command should be the following:
    sudo yum install -y cmake boost-devel libconfig-devel spdlog-devel pugixml-devel qt6-qtbase-devel
On Arch-based systems, the command should be the following:
    sudo pacman -Syu --needed cmake boost libconfig spdlog pugixml qt6-base

* Build Eliot (here in the "build" folder", with the following command:
    cmake -B build && cmake --build build --parallel 4

* Install it:
    sudo cmake --install build



3) Windows build
================

There are 2 ways to proceed:
* Cross-compilation from a Linux host, using the mingw32 cross-compiler
* Directly on Windows, using Cygwin (http://www.cygwin.com/)

Only the cross-compilation is officially supported (but adapting these
instructions for Cygwin shouldn't be too hard; patches welcome!).

Here are the steps for the cross-compilation:

* Install the build environment:
    * Go to https://github.com/mstorsjo/llvm-mingw/releases
    * Download and install the latest "UCRT" version for your platform

* Build and install dependencies:
  The Makefile in the 'extras/contrib' directory should be able to do it for you:

    cd extras/contrib && make all

  Eliot dependencies will be downloaded and cross-compiled.
  The dependencies are installed in 'extras/contrib/inst'

* Build Eliot:

    - Configure with the following command (feel free to tweak it as needed):
            export ROOT="$(pwd)"
            export INST="$ROOT/extras/contrib/inst" && \
            export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$INST/lib/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH" \
            export PKG_CONFIG_PATH_CUSTOM="$INST/lib/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH_CUSTOM" \
            cmake -B win-build \
                --toolchain "$ROOT/extras/contrib/llvm-mingw-toolchain.cmake" \
                -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH="$ROOT"

    - To compile, run 'cmake --build win-build --parallel 4' (to use 4 compilation cores)

    - Run 'cmake --build win-build -t package-win32-zip' to get everything packed up in a nice .zip



4) Mac OS X build
=================

Note: the Mac OS X build hasn't been tested since the switch to CMake.
Expect some issues and needed patches.

* Installing dependencies can be done easily using MacPorts (http://www.macports.org/):
    sudo port install cmake boost libconfig spdlog pugixml qt6-qtbase

* Configure: you can use the following command (assuming the dependencies are installed in /opt/local):
    export INST=/opt/local \
    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$INST/lib/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH" \
    cmake -B build -DENABLE_TEXT=OFF -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH="$INST"

* To compile, run 'cmake --build build --parallel 4'

* Then, to get a ready to use .dmg file, run 'make package-macosx-dmg'
